{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\np=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\");s=p.read_text().splitlines();a=next(i for i,x in enumerate(s) if x.startswith(\\\"### 4.5\\\"));b=next((i for i in range(a+1,len(s)) if s[i].startswith(\\\"## §5\\\")),len(s));print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{i+1}: {s[i]}\\\" for i in range(a,b)))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\np=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\");s=p.read_text().splitlines();a=next(i for i,x in enumerate(s) if x.startswith(\\\"### 4.5\\\"));b=next((i for i in range(a+1,len(s)) if s[i].startswith(\\\"## §5\\\")),len(s));print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{i+1}: {s[i]}\\\" for i in range(a,b)))'\"","aggregated_output":"642: ### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\n643: \n644: An earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\n645: node types: `:variable` for names bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s\n646: params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n647: free names, resolved through `resolve-var`'s store → primitives →\n648: modules fallthrough. The motivation was static inspectability: knowing a\n649: program's free-name dependencies (for UCF §7.6.1's `:yin.k/requires`\n650: fixed point, and for capability checking before running untrusted\n651: content) without evaluating it.\n652: \n653: That motivation does not require a second tag. Whether a `:variable` row\n654: is free or bound is exactly what a **query** over `$ast` (§6.1) can\n655: compute: walk the row's ancestor chain (a `node`/`nodes` slot pointing at\n656: a row's own id is a parent-child edge, so the ancestor relation is a\n657: recursive Datalog rule over the row relation itself, needing no separate\n658: occurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\n659: in any enclosing `:lambda`'s `params`. If it does not, the name is free —\n660: a `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n661: already supports recursive rules (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`'s\n662: `eval-rule`), so this is not a capability gap; it is a query the design\n663: never needed to avoid.\n664: \n665: Splitting the tag instead of querying for the same fact bought nothing\n666: the query could not already give, and cost real things: the name\n667: collided with the project's own \"no hidden global state\" invariant — the\n668: `store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n669: time-varying (it holds `yin/def` results alongside stream handles and\n670: cursor entries, §7.7.2), so the naming confusion was a real signal, not\n671: cosmetic; the hard-fail contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only resolution,\n672: throwing rather than falling through) replaced a forgiving runtime\n673: fallthrough with a requirement that every frontend get free/bound\n674: classification exactly right, which is precisely the hardest part of\n675: writing a correctly hygienic macro expander; and the change depended on\n676: frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n677: authoring time) that does not exist anywhere in this codebase. This is\n678: now a decided case study for the broader principle in\n679: [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md)'s Design Principles:\n680: before adding structure to a canonical, content-hashed representation,\n681: check whether a query already gives you the fact for free.\n682: \n683: See `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` for a working demonstration: a\n684: recursive rule (`edge`/`anc`/`depth`/`bound?`, verified against the real\n685: `dao.space.query/q` engine, not sketched) computing the free-name set of\n686: a row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n687: nearest-enclosing-binder case under shadowing.\n688: \n689: **The row-only query is unsound, not just imprecise, when a name is free\n690: at one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\n691: cost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n692: structurally identical subtrees share one row (§4.4), a name that is\n693: free at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\n694: operand `x` is free while the lambda body's `x` is bound) collapses to\n695: one `:variable` row with multiple parent edges — one through the binding\n696: lambda, one not. `bound?`'s row-level walk (`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`)\n697: finds a binding ancestor via *either* edge and calls the whole row bound,\n698: which would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\n699: `:yin.k/requires` set. UCF §7.6.1 requires that set to be a **conservative**\n700: fixed point — it must never undercount what a program depends on, since\n701: undercounting a capability requirement is a security defect, not an\n702: imprecision. The row-only query can undercount in this mixed case, so it\n703: is not sufficient for `:yin.k/requires` on its own.\n704: \n705: The fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n706: *specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\n707: §6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\n708: (`[origin root-address path ...]`, §2.5) rather than row identity: walk\n709: each occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\n710: parent edges. This is not merely argued — `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\n711: `occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\n712: against the real `q` engine, on the exact `((fn [x] x) x)` case above: a\n713: hand-built occurrence relation of §2.5's shape (the production indexer\n714: that would emit it does not exist yet, so the test builds it as fixture\n715: data, same honest scoping as the row-level demo), a recursive rule\n716: walking path-prefix ancestry rather than row edges, and — the point of\n717: the exercise — a **contrast assertion that runs the row-only rule against\n718: the same database and confirms it actually does undercount** (`#{}`\n719: instead of `#{'x}`), so the failure mode §4.5 describes above is\n720: demonstrated, not narrated. No new structure was needed; only the\n721: richer, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims. Whoever\n722: wires up the real `:yin.k/requires` computation (open item 5,\n723: \"Conservative dependency completion\") can lift that test's rule set\n724: as-is — what remains is only the indexer that emits occurrence tuples in\n725: production, not the query logic.\n726: \n727: ---\n728: \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
