ixture tags: :lambda's 3rd slot is\n  1128\t   the body, :application's 3rd the operator and 4th the operands vector.\n  1129\t   The operands edge joins every row id ([?c _ & _] matches any row, ids\n  1130\t   are always first) before member? filters, because a predicate cannot\n  1131\t   bind its own argument.\"\n  1132\t  '[[(edge ?p ?c) [?p :lambda _ ?c]]\n  1133\t    [(edge ?p ?c) [?p :application ?c _ _]]\n  1134\t    [(edge ?p ?c) [?p :application _ ?ops _] [?c _ & _] [(member? ?ops ?c)]]\n  1135\t    [(anc ?a ?d) (edge ?a ?d)]\n  1136\t    [(anc ?a ?d) (edge ?p ?d) (anc ?a ?p)]\n  1137\t    [(depth ?a ?d ?n) (edge ?a ?d) [(ground 1) ?n]]\n  1138\t    [(depth ?a ?d ?n) (edge ?p ?d) (depth ?a ?p ?m) [(inc ?m) ?n]]\n  1139\t    [(bound? ?v ?name) (anc ?l ?v) [?l :lambda ?params _] [(member? ?params ?name)]]])\n  1140\t\n  1141\t(defn- ast-row-db\n  1142\t  \"A relation value over the flat rows of ast->semantic-bytecode.\"\n  1143\t  [ast]\n  1144\t  (rel (vals (:rows (v2/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))))\n  1145\t\n  1146\t(defn- ast-row-id\n  1147\t  \"The row id of the first row matching [id tag slot1].\"\n  1148\t  [ast tag slot]\n  1149\t  (some (fn [row]\n  1150\t          (when (and (= tag (nth row 1)) (= slot (nth row 2)))\n  1151\t            (nth row 0)))\n  1152\t        (vals (:rows (v2/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))))\n  1153\t\n  1154\t(deftest recursive-rules-compute-free-names-not-tags\n  1155\t  ;; (fn [x] (+ x 1)) — §2.1: + is free, x is bound by the only :lambda.\n  1156\t  (let [ast {:type :lambda,\n  1157\t             :params ['x],\n  1158\t             :body {:type :application,\n  1159\t                    :operator {:type :variable, :name '+},\n  1160\t                    :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'x}\n  1161\t                               {:type :literal, :value 1}]}}\n  1162\t        db (ast-row-db ast)\n  1163\t        free (qq '[:find ?name :in $ %\n  1164\t                   :where\n  1165\t                   [?v :variable ?name]\n  1166\t                   (not (bound? ?v ?name))]\n  1167\t                 db free-name-rules\n  1168\t                 {:fns {'member? member?}})]\n  1169\t    (is (= #{['+]} free)\n  1170\t        \"the free-name set is exactly + ; the bound param x is absent\")))\n  1171\t\n  1172\t(deftest recursive-rules-resolve-the-nearest-enclosing-binder\n  1173\t  ;; (fn [x y] (fn [x] (+ x y))) — the inner :lambda shadows x; y is bound\n  1174\t  ;; only by the outer :lambda, two enclosing :lambdas up.\n  1175\t  (let [ast {:type :lambda,\n  1176\t             :params ['x 'y],\n  1177\t             :body {:type :lambda,\n  1178\t                    :params ['x],\n  1179\t                    :body {:type :application,\n  1180\t                           :operator {:type :variable, :name '+},\n  1181\t                           :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'x}\n  1182\t                                      {:type :variable, :name 'y}]}}}\n  1183\t        db (ast-row-db ast)\n  1184\t        opts {:fns {'member? member?}}\n  1185\t        x (ast-row-id ast :variable 'x)\n  1186\t        y (ast-row-id ast :variable 'y)\n  1187\t        outer (ast-row-id ast :lambda ['x 'y])\n  1188\t        inner (ast-row-id ast :lambda ['x])\n  1189\t        binders (fn [v nm]\n  1190\t                  (qq '[:find ?lam :in $ % ?v ?name\n  1191\t                        :where\n  1192\t                        (anc ?lam ?v)\n  1193\t                        [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n  1194\t                        [(member? ?params ?name)]]\n  1195\t                      db free-name-rules v nm opts))\n  1196\t        binder-depths (fn [v nm]\n  1197\t                        (qq '[:find ?lam ?n :in $ % ?v ?name\n  1198\t                              :where\n  1199\t                              (anc ?lam ?v)\n  1200\t                              [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n  1201\t                              [(member? ?params ?name)]\n  1202\t                              (depth ?lam ?v ?n)]\n  1203\t                            db free-name-rules v nm opts))]\n  1204\t    (is (= outer (:root (v2/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\n  1205\t        \"sanity: the params [x y] :lambda is the projected root\")\n  1206\t    (is (= #{['+]}\n  1207\t           (qq '[:find ?name :in $ %\n  1208\t                 :where\n  1209\t                 [?v :variable ?name]\n  1210\t                 (not (bound? ?v ?name))]\n  1211\t               db free-name-rules opts))\n  1212\t        \"x stays bound under shadowing and y stays bound by the outer :lambda\")\n  1213\t    (is (= #{[inner] [outer]} (binders x 'x))\n  1214\t        \"the x reference is enclosed by both shadowing :lambdas\")\n  1215\t    (is (= #{[outer]} (binders y 'y))\n  1216\t        \"y's only enclosing binder is the outer :lambda the rule had to\n  1217\t          walk past the inner one to reach\")\n  1218\t    (is (= inner (first (apply min-key second (binder-depths x 'x))))\n  1219\t        \"the nearest binder of x is the inner :lambda\")\n  1220\t    (is (= outer (first (apply min-key second (binder-depths y 'y))))\n  1221\t        \"the nearest binder of y is the outer :lambda, not the immediate\n  1222\t          :application parent\")))\n  1223\t\n  1224\t\n  1225\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  1226\t;; Occurrence-aware rules: the mixed free/bound case the row-only query\n  1227\t;; cannot resolve\n  1228\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  1229\t;; §4.5: when a name is free at one occurrence and bound at another\n  1230\t;; (((fn [x] x) x)), content addressing collapses both :variable nodes to\n  1231\t;; one row with parent edges from both places, and the row-level bound?\n  1232\t;; above finds a binder through either edge — calling the whole row bound\n  1233\t;; and silently dropping the free occurrence. The fix is a places-not-\n  1234\t;; contents query over the occurrence relation (§2.5/§6.1): free/bound is\n  1235\t;; decided per occurrence by walking that occurrence's own path to its\n  1236\t;; ancestors. No indexer here emits occurrence facts yet, so the relation\n  1237\t;; below is a hand-built fixture in §2.5's own encoding — a path is a\n  1238\t;; vector of slot steps from the root, an index into a nodes slot being a\n  1239\t;; single pair step [slot i] (which is what makes every pop of a real\n  1240\t;; path a real path, so the recursive walk never leaves the tree).\n  1241\t\n  1242\t(def ^:private path-pop\n  1243\t  \"One step up a §2.5 structural path: the parent path, or nil at the\n  1244\t   root. Supplied under :fns like member?; a fn clause may bind its\n  1245\t   output var, so this is the path-level analogue of the row-level edge.\"\n  1246\t  (fn [p] (when (pos? (count p)) (subvec p 0 (dec (count p))))))\n  1247\t\n  1248\t(def ^:private occurrence-rules\n  1249\t  \"Path-prefix ancestors and per-occurrence bound-ness. [$occ ...] names\n  1250\t   the occurrence source inside rule bodies (rule seeds carry every :in\n  1251\t   db); [$occ ?r ?lam-path ?lam] joins an ancestor occurrence's path to\n  1252\t   its row so the row relation supplies the :lambda shape.\"\n  1253\t  '[[(p-up ?child ?parent) [(path-pop ?child) ?parent]]\n  1254\t    [(occ-anc ?a ?d) (p-up ?d ?a)]\n  1255\t    [(occ-anc ?a ?d) (p-up ?d ?m) (occ-anc ?a ?m)]\n  1256\t    [(occ-bound? ?path ?name)\n  1257\t     (occ-anc ?lam-path ?path)\n  1258\t     [$occ ?r ?lam-path ?lam]\n  1259\t     [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n  1260\t     [(member? ?params ?name)]]])\n  1261\t\n  1262\t(deftest occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows\n  1263\t  ;; ((fn [x] x) x) — the lambda body's x is bound, the operand x is free,\n  1264\t  ;; and both are the SAME :variable node in the map AST.\n  1265\t  (let [ast {:type :application,\n  1266\t             :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['x],\n  1267\t                        :body {:type :variable, :name 'x}},\n  1268\t             :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'x}]}\n  1269\t        bc (v2/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)\n  1270\t        db (ast-row-db ast)\n  1271\t        x (ast-row-id ast :variable 'x)\n  1272\t        lam (ast-row-id ast :lambda '[x])\n  1273\t        ;; [root path row] for each node: the application at [], its\n  1274\t        ;; operator :lambda at [2] (operator is the application's 2nd\n  1275\t        ;; slot), the lambda's body x at [2 3] (body is :lambda's 3rd\n  1276\t        ;; slot), and the operand x at [[3 0]] (first of the :nodes slot).\n  1277\t        occ (rel [[(:root bc) [] (:root bc)]\n  1278\t                  [(:root bc) [2] lam]\n  1279\t                  [(:root bc) [2 3] x]\n  1280\t                  [(:root bc) [[3 0]] x]])\n  1281\t        opts {:fns {'member? member?, 'path-pop path-pop}}\n  1282\t        free-occ-paths (qq '[:find ?path :in $ $occ %\n  1283\t                             :where\n  1284\t                             [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n  1285\t                             [?v :variable ?name]\n  1286\t                             (not (occ-bound? ?path ?name))]\n  1287\t                           db occ occurrence-rules opts)\n  1288\t        bound-occ-paths (qq '[:find ?path :in $ $occ % ?v ?name\n  1289\t                              :where\n  1290\t                              [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n  1291\t                              (occ-bound? ?path ?name)]\n  1292\t                            db occ occurrence-rules x 'x opts)]\n  1293\t    (is (= 2 (count (filter #(= {:type :variable, :name 'x} %)\n  1294\t                            (tree-seq coll? seq ast))))\n  1295\t        \"premise, map side: the fixture really has two :variable x nodes\")\n  1296\t    (is (= #{[x]}\n  1297\t           (qq '[:find ?v :in $ ?name :where [?v :variable ?name]]\n  1298\t               db 'x))\n  1299\t        \"premise, row side: both collapse to one shared row id (§4.4)\")\n  1300\t    (is (= #{[[2 3]]} bound-occ-paths)\n  1301\t        \"of the shared row's two occurrences, only the lambda-body one is\n  1302\t          bound — the walk went up [2 3] → [2] and found the :lambda\")\n  1303\t    (is (= #{[[[3 0]]]} free-occ-paths)\n  1304\t        \"the operand occurrence [[3 0]] is free — its walk [[3 0]] → []\n  1305\t          reaches only the :application, never a binding :lambda\")\n  1306\t    (is (= #{['x]}\n  1307\t           (qq '[:find ?name :in $ $occ %\n  1308\t                 :where\n  1309\t                 [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n  1310\t                 [?v :variable ?name]\n  1311\t                 (not (occ-bound? ?path ?name))]\n  1312\t               db occ occurrence-rules opts))\n  1313\t        \"the occurrence-unioned free-name set conservatively includes x\n  1314\t          because at least one occurrence of it is free (§7.6.1)\")\n  1315\t    (is (= #{}\n  1316\t           (qq '[:find ?name :in $ %\n  1317\t                 :where\n  1318\t                 [?v :variable ?name]\n  1319\t                 (not (bound? ?v ?name))]\n  1320\t               db free-name-rules\n  1321\t               {:fns {'member? member?}}))\n  1322\t        \"contrast: on this same db the row-only rule finds the :lambda\n  1323\t          through either of the shared row's parent edges and drops x\n  1324\t          from the free-name set entirely — the §4.5 undercount\")))\n  1154\tvalue, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1\n  1155\tspecifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.\n  1156\t\n  1157\tExtraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\n  1158\ttrees):\n  1159\t\n  1160\t```clojure\n  1161\t;; Free-name extraction is a query, not a tag match (§4.5): a :variable\n  1162\t;; row's name is a free-name obligation unless it is bound by an enclosing\n  1163\t;; :lambda reached by following child-id edges from the root. This is a\n  1164\t;; recursive rule over $ast itself (child-id edges are already in the row\n  1165\t;; bodies; no separate occurrence side-table is needed) — see\n  1166\t;; `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` for the verified rule and its `:in %`\n  1167\t;; rule-set argument; the shape here is illustrative, not the exact syntax.\n  1168\t[:find ?name :in $ast % :where [$ast _ :variable ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\n  1169\t\n  1170\t;; store keys read or written by code\n  1171\t[:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\n  1172\t                                [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]\n  1173\t\n  1174\t;; FFI ops\n  1175\t[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\n  1176\t\n  1177\t;; parked ids named by resume\n  1178\t[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\n  1179\t\n  1180\t;; syntax that raises an effect: the tag, to be normalized by the footprint table below\n  1181\t[:find ?tag :in $ast\n  1182\t :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\n  1183\t        [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next\n  1184\t                      :stream/close :dao.stream.apply/call} ?tag)]]\n  1185\t```\n  1186\t\n  1187\tExtraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):\n  1188\t\n  1189\t```clojure\n  1190\t;; likewise over segment-qualified rows: derive free names from the\n  1191\t;; instruction vector's own scoping, no separate opcode needed\n  1192\t[:find ?name :in $code % :where [$code _ _ :var ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\n  1193\t[:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\n  1194\t                                  [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]\n  1195\t[:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]\n  1196\t[:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]\n  1197\t[:find ?mn   :in $code\n  1198\t :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\n  1199\t        [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\n  1200\t                      :stream-close :ffi-call} ?mn)]]\n  1280\t`:yin.k/undecodable`, the same outcome the validators give it.\n  1281\t\n  1282\t#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n  1283\t\n  1284\tA name extracted from code is an **obligation** when it is a **free**\n  1285\t`:variable` row's `name` — free per the query of §4.5, not per a\n  1286\tseparate tag. This design preserves named variables across the Semantic\n  1287\tTuple boundary, and keeps a single resolution path: the evaluator looks up\n  1288\tevery `:variable` name through `resolve-var`'s existing precedence, env →\n  1289\tstore → primitives → modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`, `ast_walker.cljc:361-363`,\n  1290\t`semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this is already what today's code does, and\n  1291\tthis design does not change it. What changes is only how the obligation\n  1292\tset is computed: not by scanning for a distinct tag, but by the §4.5/§7.7\n  1293\tquery over which `:variable` names are free.\n  1294\t\n  1295\t**Under-arity calls leave missing parameters bound to `nil`, not unbound.**\n  1296\tAn under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an\n  1297\tover-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. This\n  1298\tis a deliberate execution-contract change from today's `zipmap`-based\n  1299\tbinding (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`;\n  1300\t`semantic.cljc:200`), which currently leaves a missing parameter name\n  1301\tabsent from the extended environment rather than explicitly `nil` — so it\n  1302\tcan currently fall through to whatever the closure's own captured\n  1303\tenvironment (or, transitively, the store/primitives/modules chain) has\n  1304\tunder that name. After the fix, a missing parameter is `nil` and shadows\n  1305\tany such fallthrough within the closure's body. This is independent of\n  1306\t§4.5's free/bound decision: it is about parameter binding at closure\n  1307\tapplication, not about resolving a name to a value.\n  1308\t\n  1309\tEvery retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\n  1310\tprofile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\n  1311\tprofile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\n  1312\tcontributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\n  1313\tbecause their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\n  1314\temitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\n  1315\tpossible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\n  1316\tmakes a `:complete` result wrong.\n  1317\t\n  1318\tThis rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\n  1319\tsentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n  1320\tvalue carries\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\n  1321\tcorrect rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\n  1322\t(§10.8).\n  1323\t\n  1324\t#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\n  1325\t\n  1326\tThe unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\n  1327\taddress. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\n  1328\tenvironments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n  1329\town environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n  1330\tcontribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\n  1331\tclosure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\n  1332\tcontributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\n  1333\tname discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\n  1334\tand the iteration terminates.\n  1335\t\n  1336\t- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\n  1337\t  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\n  1338\t  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\n  1339\t- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\n  1340\t  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`\n  1341\t  naming the id.\n  1342\t- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the\n  1343\t  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait\n  1344\t  contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a\n  1345\t  closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.\n  1346\t- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets\n  1347\t  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.\n  1348\t\n  1349\t**Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:\n  1350\tthe work-item set, the obligation set, the discovered\n  1351\tvalue set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key\n  1352\trequirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization\n  1353\t(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\n  1354\tcontext, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration\n  1355\teven when no new address appeared.\n  1356\t\n  1357\tDiscovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was\n  1358\tanalyzed, every free-`:variable` obligation was discharged by the store slice by key or retained\n  1359\tas a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\n  1360\tfootprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n  1361\tfetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes\n  1362\twhere the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.\n  1363\t\n  1364\tUCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: \"repeats until nothing\n  1365\tnew is reachable\" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of \"a\n  1932\t## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n  1933\t\n  1934\t1. **The pinned canonical byte encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared and\n  1935\t   inherited, still open, but **no longer blocking ordinary identity and\n  1936\t   deduplication use** (§4.2). The three structural conformance pairs\n  1937\t   this item used to name as blocking (list/vector, seq/list, collection\n  1938\t   metadata) are closed by `dao.jing`'s committed encoder fix. What\n  1939\t   remains is the transitional hand printer's disclosed residuals\n  1940\t   (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte-array identity hashing,\n  1941\t   ambient print-var bindings on scalars — `dao.jing.md`'s Open Items)\n  1942\t   and the eventual pinned byte encoding itself, neither of which this\n  1943\t   design closes.\n  1944\t2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n  1945\t   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n  1946\t   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n  1947\t   phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain\n  1948\t   a numeric key beside the keyword one.\n  1949\t3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4) — design work\n  1950\t   with stated mechanisms, not yet implementable. Source origins\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"All seven `:global` hits are confined to §4.5. The query tests demonstrate the single-tree examples, but the occurrence rule omits root identity, so the claim that it can be reused “as-is” needs scrutiny. I also found stale row-only extraction guidance and encoder-blocking statements outside the edited sections.\n"}}
