203\t        \"the exact shape the old encoder emitted: (set (1 2))\")\n   204\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash #{1 2})\n   205\t              (jing/content-hash (list 'set [1 2])))\n   206\t        \"and its vector-tailed variant\")\n   207\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash #{}) (jing/content-hash (list 'set)))\n   208\t        \"the empty set against the empty tagged list\")\n   209\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash #{1 2}) (jing/content-hash [1 2]))\n   210\t        \"a set and the vector of its elements\")\n   211\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash #{1 2}) (jing/content-hash '(1 2)))\n   212\t        \"a set and the list of its elements\"))\n   213\t  (testing \"equal sets of any construction order still agree\"\n   214\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash #{1 2}) (jing/content-hash #{2 1})))\n   215\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash #{1 2 3}) (jing/content-hash #{3 1 2})))))\n   216\t\n   217\t\n   218\t(deftest content-hash-distinguishes-metadata\n   219\t  (testing\n   220\t    \"collection metadata is part of the address on every collection\n   221\t            branch that can carry it\"\n   222\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:a 1}))\n   223\t              (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:a 2})))\n   224\t        \"vector: differing metadata\")\n   225\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:a 1}))\n   226\t              (jing/content-hash [1 2]))\n   227\t        \"vector: metadata against none\")\n   228\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta '(1 2) {:a 1}))\n   229\t              (jing/content-hash '(1 2)))\n   230\t        \"list\")\n   231\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta (map inc [1 2]) {:a 1}))\n   232\t              (jing/content-hash (map inc [1 2])))\n   233\t        \"seq\")\n   234\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta {:a 1} {:m 1}))\n   235\t              (jing/content-hash {:a 1}))\n   236\t        \"map\")\n   237\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash (with-meta #{1} {:m 1}))\n   238\t              (jing/content-hash #{1}))\n   239\t        \"set\"))\n   240\t  (testing \"structurally equal values with equal metadata still agree\"\n   241\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash (with-meta {:a 1, :b 2} {:x 1}))\n   242\t           (jing/content-hash (with-meta {:b 2, :a 1} {:x 1})))\n   243\t        \"map order and metadata order are both normalized\")\n   244\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:x 1, :y 2}))\n   245\t           (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:y 2, :x 1})))))\n   246\t  (testing \"reader position metadata is not content\"\n   247\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash (with-meta [1 2] {:line 7, :column 11}))\n   248\t           (jing/content-hash [1 2]))\n   249\t        \"a :line/:column-tagged value addresses as its bare value\")))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defrecord RecordProbe\n   253\t  [a b])\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\t(deftest records-are-rejected-not-addressed-as-maps\n   257\t  (testing\n   552\tinside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are\n   553\tvalues.\n   554\t\n   555\t### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, and every identity use is blocked on it\n   556\t\n   557\t`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer\n   558\t(`jing.cljc`'s `order-normalize` / `canonical-print`). The three\n   559\tconformance pairs this section used to name as blocking collisions are\n   560\tnow closed:\n   561\t\n   562\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   563\t| Two different values                                   | Address today                       | Why it matters                                                           |\n   564\t+========================================================+====================================+==========================================================================+\n   565\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`              | distinct                            | `conj` appends to one and prepends to the other                          |\n   566\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   567\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (seq [1 2])]`        | distinct                            | a seq is sequential but not a list, and both differ from the vector      |\n   568\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   569\t| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1   | distinct                            | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read |\n   570\t| 2]]`                                                   |                                      | it; collection metadata is now address-significant                      |\n   571\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   572\t\n   573\t`dao.jing.md`'s Canonical encoding section records the encoder's current\n   574\tcontract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\n   575\tis not address-significant, pathological symbols whose print mimics\n   576\tanother value's can still collide, byte arrays are hashed by identity\n   577\trather than content, and ambient print-var bindings still reach scalar\n   578\tbytes. None of the three pairs above are affected by any of those\n   579\tresiduals — they were the structural (collection-level) gaps this design\n   580\tdepended on closing, and they are closed. **The identity and\n   581\tdeduplication block this section used to impose is lifted for ordinary\n   582\tvalues** — rows, vectors, ledger-record maps, flat-projection row\n   583\tcollapse (§6.1), DAG sharing (§4.4), process-local address-keyed caches,\n   584\tand `dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal value may treat\n   585\ttwo equal addresses as one value. The remaining residuals above are\n   586\tpre-existing, scoped, latent (no current producer emits scalar metadata\n   587\tor byte-array payloads), and tracked as `dao.jing.md` Open Items rather\n   588\tthan as a design-wide block — they become live concerns only if a future\n   589\tproducer starts emitting the specific value shapes they affect.\n   590\t\n   591\tThe pinned canonical byte encoding itself — the target `dao.jing.md`\n   592\tdescribes as its first open item, replacing this transitional hand\n   593\tprinter — remains open work; when it lands, every address minted under\n   594\tthis design changes with every other `dao.jing` address, the dependency\n   595\tUCF §7.3.2 already accepts.\n   596\t\n  1090\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1091\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1092\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n  1093\tids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n  1094\tregardless, so that a load taking ids as given still names the defect.\n  1095\t\n  1096\tThere is no encoder-domain rule: §4.2 blocks identity uses outright rather\n  1097\tthan admitting a domain.\n  1098\t\n  1099\t### 7.5 Vector rules\n  1100\t\n  1101\tDefects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n  1102\tdense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n  1103\tvector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n  1104\tUCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n  1105\t\n  1106\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1107\t| Rule             | Defect when                                                                                                                                         |\n  1108\t+==================+=====================================================================================================================================================+\n  1109\t| `:nonempty`      | the vector has no instructions                                                                                                                      |\n  1110\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1111\t| `:mnemonic`      | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`)                                                                         |\n  1112\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1113\t| `:arity`         | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2                                         |\n  1114\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1115\t| `:operand-kind`  | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a    |\n  1116\t|                  | a `:var` name is not a symbol, a `:closure` params is not a vector of symbols, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n  1117\t|                  | prefix is not a string                                                                                                                              |\n  1118\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1119\t| `:saturation`    | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil                                               |\n  1120\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1121\t| `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)`                                                            |\n  1122\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1123\t| `:terminator`    | the last instruction is not in `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:19-21`)                                                                               |\n  1124\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1125\t| `:argc`          | a `:call`/`:ffi-call` argc is not a non-negative integer                                                                                            |\n  1126\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1127\t\n  1128\tThere is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST, checked by §7.4; an unbound `:var` name at run time evaluates to `nil` (§7.7.2).\n  1129\t\n  1130\tThe address check (the vector hashes to the address it claims) is UCF\n  1131\t§7.3.4's and runs before these rules whenever an address is claimed; a\n  1132\tcorrect hash is never structural validation. The failure outcome of both\n646:params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n660:a `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n668:`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n676:frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n686:a row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n691:cost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n705:The fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n    46\t(defn resolve-var\n    47\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n    48\t\n    49\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n    50\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n    51\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    52\t    (val pair)\n    53\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n    54\t      (val pair)\n    55\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n    56\t        (val pair)\n    57\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n    58\t                            (module/resolve-module\n    59\t                              registry\n    60\t                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n    61\t                                           \".\"\n    62\t                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n    63\t          resolved\n    64\t          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n    65\t                {:symbol name}))))))\n    66\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"## Verdict: **No — not safe to commit as-is**\n\nThe retirement is sound in principle, but stale rules and overclaims remain.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Completeness of retirement | **FAIL** — tag references are clean; obsolete resolution semantics remain |\n| 2. §4.5’s argument and test descriptions | **FAIL** — demonstrations are valid within their fixtures, but production-readiness is overstated |\n| 3. Encoder correction | **FAIL** — §4.2’s three pairs are correctly distinguished, but contradictory blocking language and an incorrect pair remain |\n| 4. New design principle | **FAIL** — useful direction, overstated guarantees |\n\n### 1. Retirement completeness\n\n**The literal search passes:** all seven `:global` hits are inside §4.5 and discuss the retired concept.\n\nHowever, [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1128](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1128) still says:\n\n- Variable nesting is checked by §7.4, although its scope rule was removed.\n- An unbound runtime variable evaluates to `nil`.\n\nThe retained `resolve-var` instead searches env → store → primitives → modules and **throws if unresolved**, as shown at [engine.cljc:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:46). Nil-filled missing parameters are a separate case.\n\n### 2. §4.5: correct approach, incomplete soundness claim\n\n**The cited demonstrations are accurately supported by the test code:**\n\n- [query_test.cljc:1154](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1154) derives free `+` from codec-generated rows.\n- [query_test.cljc:1172](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1172) checks shadowing and selects the nearest binder.\n- [query_test.cljc:1262](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1262) uses codec-generated rows plus manually supplied occurrences, identifies the free operand `x`, and demonstrates the row-only undercount.\n\n**But three corrections are needed:**\n\n1. **The occurrence rule cannot be lifted “as-is” for multiple roots.**  \n   That claim appears at [lines 721–725](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:721). The actual `occ-bound?` rule accepts only path and name; its root variable is unconstrained: [query_test.cljc:1256](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1256).\n\n   Put `(fn [y] x)` and `(fn [x] 1)` in one occurrence database. The first tree’s free `x` can find the second tree’s binding lambda at ancestor path `[]`, incorrectly becoming bound. Thread root identity through the rule and its calls, or explicitly restrict each invocation to one root. The existing test proves only the single-root case.\n\n2. **§7.7 still prescribes the query §4.5 rejects.**  \n   [Lines 1161–1168](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1161) explicitly say no occurrence relation is needed and show name-only `bound?`. This contradicts §4.5’s mixed-occurrence analysis and §10 item 5. The segment example at [1190–1192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1190) likewise discards segment/site identity. Calling the syntax illustrative does not fix the missing scope.\n\n3. **The historical “throwing” claim is inaccurate.**  \n   [Lines 671–672](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:671) characterize the retired lexical-only contract as throwing. The pre-change HEAD text instead specified that unbound lexical variables evaluate to `nil`. Describe the actual retired contract.\n\n### 3. Encoder correction\n\n**The three concrete §4.2 pairs pass code inspection.** The encoder preserves vector brackets versus list/seq parentheses and prints retained collection metadata: [jing.cljc:45](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:45), [jing.cljc:100](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:100). Corresponding assertions appear at [jing_test.cljc:188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing_test.cljc:188) and [218](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing_test.cljc:218).\n\nRemaining defects:\n\n- **§10 misnames the second pair “seq/list.”** [Line 1937](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1937) should say **seq/vector**. Lists and equal-content seqs intentionally share an address.\n- **The lifted block remains asserted elsewhere:** the §4.2 heading at [555](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:555), §4.4 at [630](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:630), §6.1 at [877–880](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:877), and §7.4 at [1096](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1096).\n- **Residuals are not exclusively future-producer concerns.** [Lines 585–589](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:585) overstate this. Ambient print bindings affect ordinary scalar payloads already: scalars still delegate to `pr-str`. Qualify the supported encoding conditions rather than claiming every residual requires a new value shape.\n\n### 4. “Derive, don’t persist”\n\nThe principle is general and actionable, but [datom.world.md:145–152](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:145) overclaims:\n\n- Derivation is not automatically **zero storage or migration cost**; occurrence generation and consumer/query changes remain.\n- The case study requires **occurrence- and root-scoped ancestry**, not merely row reachability.\n\nKeep the preference for derived facts, while requiring correct scope and acknowledging computation, indexing, and consumer costs.\n\n**Validation:** inspected the diffs, document-wide references, query tests, and committed encoder implementation. Tests were not executed; no files were edited."}}
